> You step onto the landing. The corridor opens onto a broad landing where a great stair spirals both up into blackness and down toward a colder draft that smells of stone and old water. A tall window frames the mere below, its black water quilted with moonlight, and a Vigil Lamp on the newel-post wakes to a steady brass glow as you draw near. The banister is worn smooth by four centuries of hands; a bench sits beneath the window with a forgotten shawl folded on it. Passages run east into a hush of shut doors and west toward a faint smell of chalk, while the stair climbs up and descends down.
Exits: down, east, south, up, west.
